1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:03,410
Five minutes before your talk, you plug in,

2
00:00:03,430 --> 00:00:06,174
and the 4K projector shows a blurry 1080p.

3
00:00:06,194 --> 00:00:07,359
Or nothing at all.

4
00:00:07,379 --> 00:00:08,609
The laptop is fine.

5
00:00:08,629 --> 00:00:11,364
Somewhere in your bag is a weak link.

6
00:00:12,764 --> 00:00:15,238
Weakest Link tells you before you leave.

7
00:00:15,258 --> 00:00:17,857
Pick your laptop, tick what's in your bag,

8
00:00:17,877 --> 00:00:20,787
then choose the venue and the picture you want.

9
00:00:20,807 --> 00:00:24,848
It all runs in your browser, on Relate's WebAssembly engine.

10
00:00:26,048 --> 00:00:30,584
The answer is a path through a graph: port, hub, cable, projector input.

11
00:00:30,604 --> 00:00:33,938
The hop with the lowest capacity is the weakest link.

12
00:00:33,958 --> 00:00:36,217
Here, a hub that only does HDMI 1.4.

13
00:00:36,237 --> 00:00:39,764
Every link shows its source and a confidence level.

14
00:00:40,964 --> 00:00:43,926
Best purchase looks across every venue you visit.

15
00:00:43,946 --> 00:00:46,603
One USB-C to HDMI cable fixes three of nine.

16
00:00:46,623 --> 00:00:49,402
And when the venue's own display is the limit,

17
00:00:49,422 --> 00:00:52,400
it says so instead of selling you something.

18
00:00:53,600 --> 00:00:54,961
Same graph, in watts.

19
00:00:54,981 --> 00:00:58,250
A 140 watt charger on a thin cable charges slowly.

20
00:00:58,270 --> 00:01:02,780
It names the cable to buy, and one click later, it fast-charges.

21
00:01:03,980 --> 00:01:08,121
Your AI assistant gets the same graph through Relate's MCP server.

22
00:01:08,141 --> 00:01:10,958
This pack-my-bag agent reads a venue's email,

23
00:01:10,978 --> 00:01:14,552
writes it into its own copy of the graph with the source,

24
00:01:14,572 --> 00:01:16,695
and runs the vetted query recipes.

25
00:01:16,715 --> 00:01:21,176
Pack this, leave that, buy one cable, and a better charging cable.

26
00:01:23,876 --> 00:01:24,601
Why a graph?

27
00:01:24,621 --> 00:01:29,693
Compatibility is relationships: what plugs into what, and what each hop can carry.

28
00:01:29,713 --> 00:01:34,475
Relate keeps it all in one cited file that runs the same in the command line,

29
00:01:34,495 --> 00:01:36,649
the browser, and MCP. Weakest Link.

30
00:01:36,669 --> 00:01:39,080
Find it before your audience does.
